<p>W.W. Wood Products is now accepting orders on <strong>Cambridge</strong>, a new door style that adds a layer of detail to a familiar profile. It arrived alongside <a href="/playbook/pacifica-ventura-door-styles/">Pacifica and Ventura</a> — and where those two go slimmer and more modern, Cambridge goes richer and more traditional.</p>

<div class="post-figure">
  <img src="/images/blog/cambridge-door-style/cambridge-maple-honey.webp" alt="Cambridge cabinet door in Maple Honey stain, showing the applied moulding framing a flat center panel" width="1200" height="1200" fetchpriority="high" />
</div>

<p><em>Cambridge in Maple Honey, W.W. Wood's newest finish.</em></p>

<h2>The construction</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Cambridge is a take on Malibu</strong> with an <strong>added applied moulding</strong> on the inside edge of the frame.</li>
  <li>The applied moulding brings the <strong>rail and stile total width to 2″</strong>.</li>
  <li>It also increases the door thickness to <strong>just under 7/8″</strong>.</li>
  <li>The center is a <strong>solid, reversed flat insert panel</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where it wins</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Transitional and traditional kitchens</strong> where a plain shaker feels too simple but a raised panel feels dated — the stepped moulding adds shadow and depth without the bulk of a raised panel.</li>
  <li><strong>Libraries, offices, and built-ins</strong> where a furniture-like door is the point.</li>
  <li><strong>Stained maple.</strong> Shown in <a href="/playbook/maple-honey-stain/">Maple Honey</a>, the moulding profile picks up the stain and reads like millwork.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Before you quote</h2>

<p>Confirm species, finish, and line availability for Cambridge with your Pinnacle Sales rep or the current spec book before quoting — especially on a first order.</p>

